    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that the sum of two numbers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) is 6, i.e.,\n\\[ x + y = 6 \\]\nIt is also given that the difference of their squares is equal to 12, i.e.,\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\]\nTo solve for \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), we can factorize the difference of squares:\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\]\nInserting the given values:\n\\[ (x + y)(x - y) = 12 \\]\nWe already know that \\( x + y = 6 \\), substituting this into the equation:\n\\[ 6(x - y) = 12 \\]\n\\[ x - y = 2 \\]\nNow, we have two equations:\n\\[ x + y = 6 \\]\n\\[ x - y = 2 \\]\nAdding these two equations, we get:\n\\[ 2x = 8 \\]\n\\[ x = 4 \\]\nSubstituting \\( x \\) into the first equation, we solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 4 + y = 6 \\]\n\\[ y = 2 \\]\nTherefore, the positive difference between the two numbers is:\n\\[ x - y = 4 - 2 = 2 \\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\]",
