      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers as \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). We are given the following two equations based on the problem statement:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nWe can use the identity for the difference of squares, which states:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)\n\\]\nSubstituting the value from the first equation into this identity, we get:\n\\[\n(x + y)(x - y) = 12\n\\]\nSince \\( x + y = 6 \\), we can substitute 6 for \\( x + y \\):\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\nTo find \\( x - y \\), divide both sides by 6:\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n\\]\nThe positive difference of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
